@* Toolbar's What doesn't fit page, moved from its Overview: what moves into the … menu first, icons
   before the menu, and wrapping instead. *@

<Heading Level="2">What moves first</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Priority</Code> on an item (higher stays longer) and <Code>Overflow</Code>: <Code>Never</Code> for the main action, <Code>Always</Code> for what only the menu needs.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-priority" />
<TextBlock>With the same priority, controls move from the middle of the toolbar outward: the start from its end, then the end from its start, so the main action at the far end goes last. A <Code>ToolbarSeparator</Code> goes when there's nothing left on one side of it.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Icons before the menu</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>CollapseLabels</Code> to drop the text of buttons and menus with a <Code>StartIcon</Code> before anything moves into the … menu: they show only their icons, all at once, and the text stays for screen readers and as the tooltip. Narrow the window: once the icons alone don't fit, items move into the menu as before. One with <Code>Overflow="ToolbarOverflow.Never"</Code>, such as Publish here, keeps its text.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-collapse-labels" />

<Heading Level="2">Wrapping instead</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Wrap</Code> for a toolbar where everything must show: it wraps onto more rows, with no menu.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-wrap" />
